From 85852584aa53b6ee18b76e9b2a1acbae4c606e1d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tim 'mithro' Ansell <me@mith.ro>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 22:08:53 +0930
Subject: [PATCH] bcm2835_systmr: fire a compare the counter has just passed

The system timer matches on equality with the counter's lower 32 bits,
so a compare value the counter has already passed only matches after it
wraps, ~71 minutes later.  Linux programs "counter + delta" with a
minimum delta of 15 us (0xf ticks), which real hardware writes long
before the deadline; under emulation the vCPU can take longer than that
between reading the counter and writing the compare.  The compare then
lands just behind the counter and the guest waits over an hour for its
next tick: on raspi0 about one boot in thirty stopped in cpu_v6_do_idle
with CS=0, C3 in the past, and a trace ending

  bcm2835_systmr_run timer #3 expiring in 9842 us
  bcm2835_systmr_run timer #3 expiring in 4294967294 us

Treat a compare in the past half of the 32-bit range as already due, as
time_after() would.  This differs from the hardware only for compare
values set 35-71 minutes ahead, which Linux never programs (it keeps idle
periods well within half the counter's wrap for timekeeping).  With it,
60 resets during usb-net traffic gave 61 good boots; the trace shows 5
compares written in the past that fired immediately instead of hanging.
---
 hw/timer/bcm2835_systmr.c | 16 +++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/hw/timer/bcm2835_systmr.c b/hw/timer/bcm2835_systmr.c
index 06a919b..32599b2 100644
--- a/hw/timer/bcm2835_systmr.c
+++ b/hw/timer/bcm2835_systmr.c
@@ -73,7 +73,7 @@ static void bcm2835_systmr_write(void *opaque, hwaddr offset,
     BCM2835SystemTimerState *s = BCM2835_SYSTIMER(opaque);
     int index;
     uint32_t value = value64;
-    uint32_t triggers_delay_us;
+    int32_t triggers_delay_us;
     uint64_t now;
 
     trace_bcm2835_systmr_write(offset, value);
@@ -91,8 +91,18 @@ static void bcm2835_systmr_write(void *opaque, hwaddr offset,
         index = (offset - A_COMPARE0) >> 2;
         s->reg.compare[index] = value;
         now = qemu_clock_get_us(QEMU_CLOCK_VIRTUAL);
-        /* Compare lower 32-bits of the free-running counter. */
-        triggers_delay_us = value - now;
+        /*
+         * The hardware matches on equality with the counter's lower 32
+         * bits, so a compare value the counter has already passed only
+         * matches after it wraps, ~71 minutes later.  Guests program
+         * "counter + small delta" (Linux: at least 15 us), which real
+         * hardware writes long before the deadline; under emulation the
+         * vCPU can take longer than that between reading the counter and
+         * writing the compare, leaving it just behind and the guest
+         * waiting over an hour for its next timer tick.  Treat a compare
+         * in the past half of the 32-bit range as already due.
+         */
+        triggers_delay_us = MAX((int32_t)(value - (uint32_t)now), 0);
         trace_bcm2835_systmr_run(index, triggers_delay_us);
         timer_mod(&s->tmr[index].timer, now + triggers_delay_us);
         break;
-- 
